SDK turbo: guest durations scaled in microseconds, so sub-millisecond waits do not spin
(docs/turbo.md "Precise sleeps"). Applied by tools/build_sdk_from_source.py (SDK_BEHAVIOUR_PATCHES).
FABLE2_TURBO_FINE_DELAY (default on; =0 restores the millisecond path).

XThread::Delay (KeDelayExecutionThread), the XObject Wait / SignalAndWait / WaitMultiple
timeouts and the XTimer period converted the guest duration to whole milliseconds and then
divided by the turbo speed (Clock::ScaleGuestDurationMillis), truncating again. Under turbo a
guest Delay(1 ms) became 0.5 -> 0 ms, a yield, so five Fable II workers that sleep 1 ms in a
loop each spun a core (~580% process CPU at 2x/60 vs ~120% at 1x); a 1 ms wait timeout became
a poll that returned X_STATUS_TIMEOUT at once; a 1 ms timer period became 0, a one-shot timer.
Now, while durations are scaled (scalar != 1, clock_no_scaling off) and the switch is on, they
are scaled to microseconds (at least 1 us; 0 stays a poll, UINT32_MAX ms stays infinite):
Delay sleeps in microseconds; waits go through new rex::thread::WaitMicros /
SignalAndWaitMicros / WaitMultipleMicros (the posix wait internals are templated on the
duration type; the millisecond instantiation is the old code); Timer periods are microseconds
(milliseconds convert exactly). At 1x every caller takes the millisecond path unchanged.
Defines REX_THREAD_WAIT_MICROS in rex/thread.h.

